.TH HSH 1 "December 2025" "Simple Shell Manual"

.SH NAME
hsh \- simple UNIX command interpreter

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ./hsh
.RI [options]

.nf
#include "shell.h"
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <sys/types.h>
#include <sys/wait.h>
#include <sys/stat.h>
.fi

.B int simple_shell(void);

.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBhsh\fR  is a custom UNIX shell command interpreter written by Enzo MESSAOUDI and Vincent RENAUD (C#28-Sens).

It behaves like a standard shell and supports various system calls, functions and command execution.

It reads commands from standard input, parses them into arguments, and executes them using the underlying UNIX system calls.

.PP
The following conversion specifiers are supported:

.RS
.IP "String Manipulation and Memory Handling"
All functions from \fIstring.h\fR
.IP "File & Directory Operations"
access, chdir, open, close, read, write, stat, lstat, fstat, getcwd
.IP "Directory Stream Functions"
opendir, readdir, closedir
.IP "Process Control"
fork, execve, exit, _exit, getpid
.IP "Process Synchronization / Waiting"
wait, waitpid, wait3, wait4
.IP "Signals"
signal, kill
.IP "Memory Management"
malloc, free
.IP "Standard I/O"
printf, fprintf, vfprintf, sprintf, putchar, fflush, perror, getline
.IP "Terminal & Strings"
isatty, strtok
.RE

.SH USAGE
Run the shell executable:


 ./hsh
displays a prompt "$ " and waits for user input. Commands are tokenized by spaces and executed using
.B execve(2).
.PP
The shell loop continues until the user enters the built-in
.B exit
command or an end-of-file (Ctrl+D) is encountered.


.SH COMMAND PARSING
The shell splits each input line into an array of arguments (argv) using spaces as delimiters. A maximum of 9 arguments per command is supported; the array is NULL-terminated.

.SH EXECUTION
Each command is executed in a child process created using
.B fork(2).
The child process attempts to execute the command with
.B execve(2).
If execution fails, an error message is printed using
.B perror(3),
and the child exits with status 127. The parent process waits for the child to finish.

.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Start the shell
$ hsh
.TP
Execute a command
$ ls -l /tmp
.TP
Exit the shell
$ exit

.SH EXIT STATUS
The \fB./hsh\fR shell returns an exit status to indicate the result of the last command executed or the shell itself. The possible values are:

.RS
.TP
0
The shell exited successfully or the last command executed successfully.
.TP
1
A generic error occurred within the shell, such as an internal failure or invalid input.
.TP
126
A command was found but could not be executed.
.TP
127
A command was not found.

